TASK AND OBJECTIVE
Build RETIS Voice mode as a hands-free voice conversation screen in the style of ChatGPT's voice mode: a centered circular orb with moving colour inside it, a microphone button and a close button. It must feel instant, hear everything the user says, change the orb's colour for each phase of the conversation, and stop speaking the moment the user interrupts.
Orb behavior by state (colour and motion are driven by the real state, never faked):
Idle or muted: calm, slow breathing, neutral colour.
User speaking (listening): colour A, with the inner swirl moving more or less according to the real microphone level.
RETIS thinking (request pending): colour B, a slow steady swirl.
RETIS speaking: colour C, a distinct flowing swirl while speech is really playing.
Interrupted: colour returns to A within one frame, as soon as the user's voice is detected.
Use three clearly distinguishable colours. They must differ in hue and not only in brightness, and a state must also be conveyed by the status label and motion, so colour is never the only signal.
Entry and exit: the voice (waveform) button in the chat composer opens Voice mode. The microphone starts listening immediately, with no second press. The close button or Esc leaves it. This replaces the earlier composer-dictation behavior and supersedes all earlier voice specifications (the video overlay, the persistent composer toggle and the speech-latency prompt). Where this conflicts with an earlier instruction, this one wins. The telemetry freshness, grounding and speed requirements from those earlier prompts are restated below and still apply.
All RETIS answers stay grounded in real IRIS data. Never fabricate a measurement to avoid an "unavailable" answer. If the sensor genuinely has no reading, RETIS says so briefly and honestly. Everything else about the experience must be fast and free of errors visible to the user.
