IMPLEMENTATION REQUIREMENTS
Screen layout
Voice mode is an overlay filling the RETIS container below the top navigation. It is not a route. The page never scrolls.
Centered column: the orb, then one status line, then a caption area, then the controls row. Nothing else.
Orb: a circle sized clamp(160px, 28vh, 260px). Below 700px viewport height use 140px. Below 560px use 120px.
Controls row: a microphone button (mute toggle, 56px) and a close button (44px), 16px apart, icon-only, with aria-labels. The microphone is live by default. Pressing it mutes input (crossed-out glyph, aria-pressed), pressing again unmutes. The crossed-out microphone shows only when the user has muted or when the microphone is blocked or unavailable. Never show it for an internal restart or pause. Closing (X or Esc) ends the session.
Status line, one line, secondary colour, aria-live="polite", derived from the real state only: "Listening", "Thinking", "Speaking", "Muted", and for rare recoveries "Reconnecting microphone...". No placeholder labels such as "Notice".
Caption area, below the status line: at most two lines. While the user speaks, show the live transcript in a secondary style. While RETIS speaks, show the reply text being spoken, in full-contrast text, with the words not yet spoken at 35% opacity and the spoken words at full opacity (opacity changes only, 140ms). Use word-boundary events when available, otherwise estimate from audio duration. A caption toggle (small icon button) hides captions, and the choice persists. Captions fade between turns (180ms).
The orb (visual and animation specification)
Construction: a circular container with overflow hidden, containing 3 to 4 large soft blobs (circles or ellipses with a CSS or SVG radial gradient, blurred at most 24px) layered over a base fill. The blobs drift and rotate inside the circle using transform animations only (translate, rotate, scale). The orb needs no images or video. Do not use canvas or WebGL unless CSS and SVG cannot meet the frame rate, and report why.
Colours come from state tokens, defined once for all four theme combinations:
listening: a blue family
thinking: a neutral slate-blue family at lower saturation
speaking: a violet family
Derive the exact values from the existing palette and tokens, and measure contrast of the orb edge and the controls against the page. Keep the orb distinct from the page background in all four combinations (Minimal Light, Minimal Dark, Vivid Light, Vivid Dark). Do not let the Color Studio accent override these state colours.
Colour changes between states by crossfading the blob gradients over 240ms (opacity only on stacked layers), never by animating a filter or snapping.
Level reactivity: when listening, scale the swirl speed and the orb scale (between 1.0 and 1.08) with the real microphone level from an analyser on the actual microphone stream. When speaking, use the real output level when the audio source exposes one (decoded Gemini TTS audio through an analyser). The browser speech engine exposes no level, so in that case use a steady speaking swirl at fixed intensity, which is a state animation and must not imitate a level. Never display a simulated audio level.
Frame budget: animate transform and opacity only, on a compositor layer (will-change: transform on the blob layers only). Keep the animation at 60fps on the target machine while webcam monitoring runs. Measure CPU and frame times, and report them. Pause all orb animation when the window is hidden.
Style treatment: Vivid keeps a bold outline ring around the orb and a hard offset shadow. Minimal uses a fine ring and a soft shadow with the existing blur token. No glows, sparkles, particles or extra rings.
Reduced motion: no swirl or scale. Show a static orb with the state colour, and rely on the status label and caption for state. Colour changes become instant.
Hearing everything (speech input)
Hold one recognition instance, created when Voice mode mounts, running continuous with interim results. Start it synchronously inside the click handler of the voice button, before awaiting anything, so it keeps the user-gesture context. Request the microphone with echo cancellation, noise suppression and auto gain control enabled.
Browser recognition ends on its own after silence or a time limit. Restart immediately on any unexpected end (the shortest safe delay once the guard state is idle, target 100ms or less). The restart must be invisible: no status change, no orb change. Never drop words across a restart. Measure the restart gap, and test by speaking continuously for 60 seconds across at least three automatic restarts. Report the number of missed or truncated words observed, or state that this was not run.
Merge interim and final results correctly so no text is repeated or lost. Never discard audio-driven text because a request is pending: keep recognizing while RETIS is thinking or speaking (see section 5).
Exactly one instance, with the guard state (idle, starting, running, stopping), an intentional-stop flag so app-initiated stops are never shown as errors, backoff on repeated failures (500ms, 1s, 2s) and a maximum of five consecutive failures before showing a calm "Voice input stopped. Tap the microphone to start again." Never loop endlessly.
Turn detection: treat the utterance as complete when final text has not changed for 700ms (one named constant, reported), with a minimum of two recognized words, or one recognized word when it is a clear command such as "stop". Do not cut a sentence on a short pause. Report whether 700ms truncates sentences in testing.
If the recognition engine cannot meet the "hear everything" requirement (words lost at restarts beyond the threshold you report, unsupported in the packaged Electron build, or not working in the browser preview), do not silently switch to an engine that sends audio to a cloud service. Report the measured problem, and stop at that point so the user can decide. Disable the entry button with an honest message in any build where voice input cannot work.
Privacy: audio is never stored. Report where audio goes for the engine in use. A persistent visible indicator shows while the microphone is open. Request microphone permission only on the first press, with a clear explanation. If the microphone is unused for 10 minutes, end Voice mode and say so.
Answering fast, from real data
Build a fresh snapshot at the moment each utterance is sent, from the single authoritative telemetry store, through the same snapshot builder the dashboard and text chat use. Never create it when Voice mode opens or while the user is speaking, and never reuse a snapshot across turns. Cached aggregates are allowed only when keyed to the store version and the observed-minute counter and invalidated on change, and live fields (blink rate, distance, alert counts, observed time, timestamps) are always read fresh. Target 50ms or less to build.
Local fast path (no model call): reuse the existing deterministic intent matcher or add it, for direct single-metric questions about the present or today: blink rate, screen distance, blink alerts, distance warnings, longest session, total validated blinks, and fatigue score only if the app already computes it. Anything else, anything ambiguous, multi-metric, explanatory or out of scope falls through to Gemini. The reply is a fixed template filled through the existing token renderer from the fresh snapshot, and it is spoken and captioned like any reply. Do not label it "AI-generated summary", and save it to history with source = "local". Prompt-injection text must fall through and never change a value. Unit tests cover matches, near-misses and injection.
Gemini path: keep the system instruction, schema and validation unchanged. Put the static prefix first, then the snapshot, then history and the new message. Send the last 4 turns of history in token form and a compact snapshot. Mark voice-originated requests as response mode "short" (a one-line headline plus at most two sentences) in the request context. Cap output length with a named constant (about 300 tokens). Minimize reasoning effort where the SDK supports it. Use the fastest model of the existing Gemini family that passes the grounding and out-of-scope tests, keep the model name in one config constant, and report latency and any test failures for each model tried. Reuse the HTTP connection and warm it up (no user data and no generation request). Never speak or caption unvalidated text.
Spoken output: split the validated spoken string into sentences, request speech for the first sentence immediately, and start playing it as soon as audio is ready while later sentences are requested and queued, with no gaps or overlap. Show captions as speech starts. Use Gemini TTS with the browser voice as the automatic fallback: if Gemini TTS has no audio within 1500ms (one named constant), start the browser voice for that reply and discard the late audio. Spoken text is the validated reply text only, with units expanded ("blinks per minute", "centimeters"). Do not send the user's name, user audio or raw telemetry to the speech service. Do not store generated audio.
Time targets (measure, and report medians and worst cases over at least 10 runs, labelling anything not run): press to listening 300ms or less (warm, excluding the permission dialog); end of utterance to request sent 100ms or less; local fast path reply audio start within about 600ms of the final transcript; time from the end of utterance to the first spoken sentence for the Gemini path, reported per step. If the model call is the floor, say so with numbers. Do not weaken validation to hit a target.
Never present a delay as a failure. While a request is pending, the orb shows the thinking state within 100ms of the end of the utterance. Do not add fake delay.
Interruption (barge-in)
The user can interrupt RETIS at any time: while it is speaking, while the reply is queued, or while a request is pending.
Detect interruption with two signals, whichever comes first: (a) the real microphone level, measured after echo cancellation, stays above an adaptive threshold for 200ms (calibrate the noise floor during the first second of the session and keep updating it while RETIS is silent, and never let RETIS's own speaker output trigger it), and (b) recognition produces an interim result with at least one word while RETIS is speaking.
On interruption: stop speech playback immediately (target within 150ms of detection), flush the speech queue, abort in-flight speech requests with AbortController, switch the orb to the listening colour in the same frame, and keep listening. If a request is pending, abort it, and use the new utterance as the next message.
The interrupted reply stays in the chat history with its validated text and an "interrupted" flag. When history is sent to the model later, mark the turn as interrupted so the model does not assume the user heard all of it. Never delete content.
Echo: with speakers, RETIS's own voice must not be recognized or trigger interruption. Rely on getUserMedia echo cancellation, plus ignoring recognition results whose text matches the reply being spoken within the playback window. Test with laptop speakers and with headphones. Report any case where echo triggered a false interruption, and the threshold used. Recommend headphones in the report if speaker echo cannot be fully removed. Do not disable interruption to avoid the problem.
A single short sound (cough, click) must not stop RETIS: require the 200ms sustained level, or at least one recognized word.
History and grounding
Each turn saves to the chat history as a voice turn: the user transcript, the validated reply in token form with the frozen values shown at that time, metrics_used, data period, the interrupted flag, and source ("local" or "model"). Never store audio. Closing Voice mode returns to the chat, where the thread shows these turns as normal bubbles and report cards.
Every number spoken, captioned or stored comes from the snapshot built at send time. Unknown metric IDs, unknown actions and unsourced numbers are rejected as before. Out-of-scope requests speak the fixed redirect: "I can only help with your screen habits and eye comfort, such as blink rate, screen distance, alerts, breaks and the 20-20-20 rule. Try asking me about one of those."
Telemetry freshness (bug fix, re-applied): live values must never go stale during an open session. Voice mode may stay open for a long time, and every answer reflects the latest valid measurement. If any real-time voice connection exists in the project, give it an on-demand tool returning the current snapshot and keep the connection open without restarting it per update. Snapshot fields: metric id, value, unit, measurement timestamp, age in seconds, stale or unavailable flag, reliability flag and observed-time basis, using the existing staleness constant. If a value is stale or unavailable, RETIS says so briefly. Never invent a value, never substitute zero. If a metric changes while a request is pending, make sure the older result cannot overwrite newer state. Add development-only timestamped diagnostics (sensor update, store update, snapshot built with the value supplied, request sent, reply received, speech start) with a non-sensitive session id, and gate or remove them afterwards. Do not log audio, frames, keys or personal data.
Errors and recovery (nothing alarming)
No raw error codes anywhere. Recoverable events (recognition ending, a dropped TTS request, a network blip) recover silently with the backoff described above and never change the orb to an error look.
Unrecoverable cases show one calm line in the status area and one clear action: microphone blocked ("Microphone access is blocked" plus how to allow it, with a retry button), no microphone found, speech service unreachable (offer to close and use text), unsupported platform ("Voice input isn't available here"). In these cases the orb returns to the idle colour and the crossed-out microphone is shown.
Offline or a failed Gemini request: speak and caption a short honest line ("I couldn't reach the AI service. Here are your latest readings.") followed by the available live values from the snapshot, never fabricated text.
Empty or unintelligible audio sends nothing and shows no error.
Theming and accessibility
Use the existing tokens for the four theme combinations. Minimal screens keep to neutral surfaces, so the orb is the only colourful element on this screen. Vivid keeps bold outlines and hard shadows around the orb and controls. The Color Studio accent applies to focus rings and the active microphone ring only. Red stays reserved for proximity warnings.
Every control is keyboard-operable with a visible focus ring. Space toggles mute when focus is not in a text field. Esc closes. State changes are announced through the aria-live status line, not on every restart. Respect prefers-reduced-motion. Measure contrast at 4.5:1 for normal text and 3:1 for large text and UI boundaries.
